1
00:00:03,461 --> 00:00:06,757
On the small rooftop garden,
the wind blows again today.

2
00:00:06,762 --> 00:00:09,315
The tomatoes have not turned red yet.

3
00:00:09,711 --> 00:00:12,635
Well then, let's begin today's story.

4
00:00:20,128 --> 00:00:21,585
Morning.

5
00:00:22,555 --> 00:00:24,804
Just a little more water, okay?

6
00:00:27,124 --> 00:00:28,045
You're drooping.

7
00:00:28,045 --> 00:00:29,041
Can you hear me?

8
00:00:29,122 --> 00:00:30,093
...Yeah.

9
00:00:30,170 --> 00:00:32,836
Your leaves are stiffer than yesterday.

10
00:00:35,156 --> 00:00:36,430
I won't let you die.

11
00:00:36,491 --> 00:00:38,699
I mean it. You're not dying on me.

12
00:00:38,699 --> 00:00:40,120
Listen.

13
00:00:40,163 --> 00:00:43,938
We only have three weeks. Starting
today, I'll take proper care of you.

14
00:00:44,011 --> 00:00:46,589
The principal said it himself.

15
00:00:46,641 --> 00:00:48,430
That this roof is becoming solar panels.

16
00:00:48,439 --> 00:00:49,715
No way.

17
00:00:50,330 --> 00:00:51,866
It's our place.

18
00:00:51,866 --> 00:00:52,935
I'll keep at it.

19
00:00:53,044 --> 00:00:53,830
Watering's done.

20
00:00:53,830 --> 00:00:54,890
Hang in there.

21
00:00:54,979 --> 00:00:56,654
Sun's up.

22
00:00:59,692 --> 00:01:01,161
See? It's getting brighter.

23
00:01:01,185 --> 00:01:04,870
Nobody in the club has given up,
not me, not Riku, not anyone.

24
00:01:04,939 --> 00:01:06,416
And you'd better not give up either.

25
00:01:06,416 --> 00:01:07,064
Got it?

26
00:02:39,970 --> 00:02:41,246
You're going up there again?

27
00:02:43,564 --> 00:02:45,689
Of course! She's waiting for me.

28
00:02:45,788 --> 00:02:48,145
She isn't waiting. She's dead.

29
00:02:48,939 --> 00:02:49,982
She is not!

30
00:02:49,986 --> 00:02:52,330
Look at her leaves. Every
single one is limp.

31
00:02:52,330 --> 00:02:54,546
They're just droopy. She's
parched, that's all.

32
00:02:54,546 --> 00:02:56,609
We've been watering her for three days.

33
00:02:56,682 --> 00:02:58,548
Then we give her more!

34
00:02:59,142 --> 00:03:01,960
If the roots are rotting,
more water makes it worse.

35
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:03,100
Trust me.

36
00:03:03,956 --> 00:03:04,860
Ehh...

37
00:03:04,885 --> 00:03:06,401
Did you actually look at the roots?

38
00:03:06,401 --> 00:03:08,153
I don't need to. I can tell.

39
00:03:10,926 --> 00:03:12,174
You can't tell!

40
00:03:12,606 --> 00:03:16,548
Last year was the same.
Same way, same wilt.

41
00:03:19,591 --> 00:03:21,086
Last year is last year!

42
00:03:23,729 --> 00:03:26,225
Every year it's the same. What dies, dies.

43
00:03:26,225 --> 00:03:27,436
Nothing's changed.

44
00:03:27,466 --> 00:03:28,367
Riku, that's mean.

45
00:03:28,461 --> 00:03:29,829
It's a fact.

46
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:33,238
It isn't! She's still breathing!

47
00:03:33,325 --> 00:03:35,267
Plants don't breathe, you know.

48
00:03:35,361 --> 00:03:36,436
They do!

49
00:03:37,243 --> 00:03:39,018
Yeah, yeah.

50
00:03:41,432 --> 00:03:44,102
Did you touch the stem? It's still firm.

51
00:03:46,024 --> 00:03:46,962
It's firm because it's dried out.

52
00:03:46,962 --> 00:03:47,917
Dead wood feels like that.

53
00:03:47,934 --> 00:03:51,318
Then it can come back, right?

54
00:03:51,432 --> 00:03:52,356
It won't.

55
00:03:52,417 --> 00:03:54,244
It will! Absolutely will!

56
00:03:55,674 --> 00:03:57,701
Why are you so sure?

57
00:03:57,701 --> 00:04:02,477
Because Haru hasn't given
up, so neither will we.

58
00:04:02,477 --> 00:04:03,919
...

59
00:04:04,326 --> 00:04:06,370
Come on, help us out.

60
00:04:06,869 --> 00:04:08,961
I told you, I'm not coming.

61
00:04:09,247 --> 00:04:10,913
Fine. I'll do it alone, then.

62
00:04:10,967 --> 00:04:12,296
Do what you want.

63
00:04:19,472 --> 00:04:21,917
Everyone, please gather.

64
00:04:22,234 --> 00:04:23,410
Principal?

65
00:04:24,071 --> 00:04:27,740
After the spring festival, the
roof becomes solar panels.

66
00:04:27,819 --> 00:04:29,000
Huh?

67
00:04:29,091 --> 00:04:31,284
The roof is our garden!

68
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:32,887
It's decided.

69
00:04:32,985 --> 00:04:33,992
There are no exceptions.

70
00:04:34,096 --> 00:04:40,699
Unless the club proves it grows
something useful, the plan is scrapped.

71
00:04:50,197 --> 00:04:51,669
Prove it?

72
00:04:51,785 --> 00:04:54,736
What counts as useful?
What's the standard?

73
00:04:55,100 --> 00:04:55,897
Something people can eat.

74
00:04:55,897 --> 00:04:57,310
Something you can actually harvest.

75
00:04:57,344 --> 00:05:00,154
The deadline is the day
before the spring festival.

76
00:05:00,331 --> 00:05:01,745
Wait!

77
00:05:02,155 --> 00:05:06,237
We only have three weeks! Starting
from seedlings won't make it in time.

78
00:05:06,272 --> 00:05:09,859
What you can do in three
weeks is for you to work out.

79
00:05:10,457 --> 00:05:11,411
That's all I have to say.

80
00:05:11,465 --> 00:05:13,524
Do you like tomatoes?

81
00:05:13,568 --> 00:05:16,143
...We're discussing work.

82
00:05:16,254 --> 00:05:17,151
I see.

83
00:05:17,215 --> 00:05:17,922
Water on schedule.

84
00:05:17,922 --> 00:05:19,615
Keep your voices down up here.

85
00:05:19,752 --> 00:05:22,370
Complaints come in when it's loud.

86
00:05:22,380 --> 00:05:23,810
Yes.

87
00:05:24,329 --> 00:05:26,970
...But, Principal.

88
00:05:28,144 --> 00:05:29,031
What is it?

89
00:05:29,081 --> 00:05:30,006
We'll keep it.

90
00:05:30,006 --> 00:05:30,767
I promise.

91
00:05:30,888 --> 00:05:32,521
I'm not expecting much.

92
00:05:32,521 --> 00:05:33,600
Wait!

93
00:05:33,697 --> 00:05:37,874
After the deadline, the
panels go up. No exceptions.

94
00:05:41,312 --> 00:05:42,519
Excuse me.

95
00:05:42,867 --> 00:05:44,089
Hey...

96
00:05:50,235 --> 00:05:51,607
I tested the soil.

97
00:05:51,636 --> 00:05:53,109
Here are the results.

98
00:05:54,762 --> 00:05:56,748
Technically, it's not good.

99
00:05:56,748 --> 00:05:58,709
The soil is the problem.

100
00:05:58,709 --> 00:06:00,459
I'll start with the numbers.

101
00:06:01,807 --> 00:06:03,016
The pH is 6.8.

102
00:06:03,045 --> 00:06:04,940
What's pH? Is it tasty?

103
00:06:05,429 --> 00:06:09,458
It's not food. It's a number
for acid or alkaline.

104
00:06:09,559 --> 00:06:12,562
That's your favorite topic, isn't it?

105
00:06:13,483 --> 00:06:16,145
It's not a favorite. It's necessary data.

106
00:06:17,953 --> 00:06:23,427
The real problem is drainage. Pouring a hundred
milliliters took eleven minutes to soak in.

107
00:06:23,489 --> 00:06:24,513
What does "drains" mean?

108
00:06:24,601 --> 00:06:27,028
How fast water runs out of the soil.

109
00:06:27,310 --> 00:06:28,205
Run out... like a snake shedding?

110
00:06:28,205 --> 00:06:29,277
Like skin falling off, right?

111
00:06:29,329 --> 00:06:31,750
That's not what it means.

112
00:06:31,795 --> 00:06:35,394
Healthy soil takes one to three
minutes. Eleven is far too slow.

113
00:06:35,485 --> 00:06:36,860
What's happening to the roots?

114
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:40,341
The soil is packed hard. My finger
stopped at twelve centimeters.

115
00:06:40,341 --> 00:06:42,707
The roots can't grow through it.

116
00:06:42,905 --> 00:06:47,362
Technically, the roots
can't get enough oxygen.

117
00:06:48,654 --> 00:06:52,692
Oxygen! It can't breathe?
Sorry, Tomato-san!

118
00:07:08,932 --> 00:07:11,107
Don't name it.

119
00:07:11,594 --> 00:07:13,325
So what do we do?

120
00:07:13,325 --> 00:07:20,789
First we break up the soil with sand and compost.
We need at least three drainage holes too.

121
00:07:20,888 --> 00:07:23,201
Just poke some holes, how hard can it be?

122
00:07:24,181 --> 00:07:27,399
The position and number matter,
or water pools at the bottom.

123
00:07:27,399 --> 00:07:29,242
Yes, Miss Calculator.

124
00:07:29,352 --> 00:07:31,959
It's not a hobby. It's responsibility.

125
00:07:32,441 --> 00:07:34,498
Making holes in them seems mean!

126
00:07:34,506 --> 00:07:36,194
Let's do it today.

127
00:07:36,194 --> 00:07:38,866
Right, I'll log the numbers later.

128
00:07:39,364 --> 00:07:42,242
A log! I'll use my cutest notebook!

129
00:07:42,341 --> 00:07:44,247
...Do whatever you want.

130
00:07:44,325 --> 00:07:47,506
Technically, that's the best
idea anyone's had today.

131
00:07:56,319 --> 00:07:59,383
Sensei, if you're there, help us!

132
00:07:59,383 --> 00:08:01,175
I'm here. I've been here the whole time.

133
00:08:01,270 --> 00:08:02,418
Since when?!

134
00:08:02,950 --> 00:08:05,532
Since you started watering it at dawn.

135
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:07,043
...You could've said something.

136
00:08:07,093 --> 00:08:09,994
If I said something, you'd make me help.

137
00:08:11,437 --> 00:08:14,585
Obviously! It's your job!

138
00:08:16,411 --> 00:08:17,831
Job, huh.

139
00:08:18,456 --> 00:08:20,531
Did you look at the roots?

140
00:08:20,624 --> 00:08:22,458
I did. You don't need to look to know.

141
00:08:22,465 --> 00:08:24,514
We've been watering it every day!

142
00:08:24,560 --> 00:08:27,129
It's not a water problem.
It's a root problem.

143
00:08:27,202 --> 00:08:28,002
Roots?

144
00:08:28,059 --> 00:08:29,870
That's exactly it. The
roots can't breathe.

145
00:08:29,870 --> 00:08:31,205
Breathe...?

146
00:08:31,384 --> 00:08:33,878
The soil's too packed. Water
pools and the roots drown.

147
00:08:33,878 --> 00:08:35,206
Nothing can grow in that.

148
00:08:35,235 --> 00:08:36,306
Drowning...

149
00:08:36,966 --> 00:08:39,378
Technically, that's correct.

150
00:08:39,468 --> 00:08:41,180
Koizumi, how long were you listening?

151
00:08:41,235 --> 00:08:43,077
I came to report, and
Sensei was already talking.

152
00:08:43,077 --> 00:08:43,943
He beat me to it.

153
00:08:44,013 --> 00:08:46,049
Hmm. So what do we do?

154
00:08:46,068 --> 00:08:47,803
Break up the soil, then repot it.

155
00:08:47,803 --> 00:08:50,051
Be careful not to hurt the roots.

156
00:08:50,230 --> 00:08:52,159
Boring work.

157
00:08:53,616 --> 00:08:55,754
The boring part matters most.

158
00:08:55,769 --> 00:08:59,241
...You said that before, didn't you?

159
00:08:59,892 --> 00:09:02,337
I did. I'll keep saying it.

160
00:09:02,479 --> 00:09:03,830
Will you help?

161
00:09:03,903 --> 00:09:05,813
Hmm. I have one condition.

162
00:09:06,970 --> 00:09:08,080
Condition?

163
00:09:08,286 --> 00:09:10,919
Melon bread, and one won't be enough.

164
00:09:11,974 --> 00:09:14,075
There it is, the melon bread racket.

165
00:09:14,118 --> 00:09:17,423
Don't call it a racket. It's a fair trade.

166
00:09:19,204 --> 00:09:20,351
Wait.

167
00:09:22,490 --> 00:09:25,224
We'll buy it. You just watch from there.

168
00:09:25,242 --> 00:09:28,697
Even watching wears me out, you know.

169
00:09:35,693 --> 00:09:37,694
Owada, why did you quit?

170
00:09:37,811 --> 00:09:39,127
Where's that coming from all of a sudden?

171
00:09:39,763 --> 00:09:40,665
It's about last year.

172
00:09:40,766 --> 00:09:42,644
No reason. I just got bored.

173
00:09:45,750 --> 00:09:47,046
Liar.

174
00:09:47,217 --> 00:09:48,129
Don't be annoying.

175
00:09:48,175 --> 00:09:49,756
Do you remember the last tomato?

176
00:09:49,869 --> 00:09:51,188
...Yeah, I remember.

177
00:09:51,245 --> 00:09:56,930
They all split before they
ripened. Watering was my job.

178
00:09:57,174 --> 00:09:59,515
I checked them every day
and still missed it.

179
00:09:59,629 --> 00:10:01,314
So that's why you quit?

180
00:10:01,329 --> 00:10:03,355
"Quit" is a nice word for it. I ran.

181
00:10:03,355 --> 00:10:04,492
Oh...

182
00:10:07,863 --> 00:10:09,430
I don't think running is the right word.

183
00:10:09,529 --> 00:10:10,676
Don't bother comforting me.

184
00:10:11,418 --> 00:10:12,007
I'm not.

185
00:10:12,007 --> 00:10:14,003
Just stating facts.

186
00:10:14,106 --> 00:10:15,403
...Hmph.

187
00:10:15,602 --> 00:10:17,493
We didn't get to eat a single one.

188
00:10:17,493 --> 00:10:19,334
That was the worst part.

189
00:10:19,939 --> 00:10:23,134
You still like tomatoes, don't you, Owada?

190
00:10:23,275 --> 00:10:24,039
I don't like them.

191
00:10:24,039 --> 00:10:25,494
I just can't stop caring.

192
00:10:25,551 --> 00:10:27,411
So will you help me?

193
00:10:27,507 --> 00:10:29,043
...Fine. I'll help.

194
00:10:29,093 --> 00:10:30,784
But I have a request.

195
00:10:30,795 --> 00:10:33,436
Don't tell anyone about this.

196
00:10:34,243 --> 00:10:36,725
Especially Tomoe. She'll
make a huge deal out of it.

197
00:10:36,725 --> 00:10:38,001
Why her?

198
00:10:38,039 --> 00:10:40,130
I don't want anyone to know
I'm the one who lost them.

199
00:10:40,130 --> 00:10:40,668
Please.

200
00:10:43,315 --> 00:10:45,202
Okay. I promise.

201
00:10:45,693 --> 00:10:47,900
I won't tell anyone. Never.

202
00:10:47,918 --> 00:10:49,894
But you have to show up too, okay?

203
00:10:49,935 --> 00:10:53,011
If you don't come, I'll drag
you up to the roof myself.

204
00:10:54,735 --> 00:10:56,319
Fine, fine.

205
00:11:13,695 --> 00:11:14,741
Whoa, these are heavy!

206
00:11:14,777 --> 00:11:16,810
Hey, you're not carrying two bags alone.

207
00:11:16,836 --> 00:11:18,036
I'm fine. I can handle it.

208
00:11:18,065 --> 00:11:21,383
If you trip on these stairs, you'll
be covered in soil. Seriously, stop.

209
00:11:21,383 --> 00:11:22,439
Oh. Okay.

210
00:11:22,476 --> 00:11:24,009
Hey, look at this pot!

211
00:11:24,022 --> 00:11:26,496
I already named it. Pottun.

212
00:11:26,519 --> 00:11:27,815
Pottun? What kind of name is that?

213
00:11:27,832 --> 00:11:28,958
Isn't it cute?

214
00:11:32,044 --> 00:11:33,672
Cute for a pot? That's a stretch.

215
00:11:33,712 --> 00:11:35,823
Then what would you call it, Riku?

216
00:11:36,041 --> 00:11:36,626
Broken Pot.

217
00:11:36,626 --> 00:11:38,464
Because it's definitely broken.

218
00:11:38,573 --> 00:11:39,412
Rude.

219
00:11:39,412 --> 00:11:41,248
I'm just stating facts.

220
00:11:41,948 --> 00:11:43,795
And this soil bag is Mogu-mogu.

221
00:11:44,915 --> 00:11:47,191
Soil can't eat anything, Riku.

222
00:11:47,381 --> 00:11:49,466
I haven't figured out
what the name means yet.

223
00:11:49,466 --> 00:11:51,535
Let's just get these up first.

224
00:11:51,808 --> 00:11:52,855
Hehe, Mogu-mogu.

225
00:11:52,855 --> 00:11:54,526
Torii, stop talking on the stairs.

226
00:11:54,553 --> 00:11:56,355
Everyone, stop right where you are.

227
00:11:56,435 --> 00:12:00,418
You're lifting with your back, which is
how people get hurt. Bend your knees.

228
00:12:02,965 --> 00:12:04,605
Pull the bag in close to your body.

229
00:12:04,632 --> 00:12:08,316
In theory, that cuts the strain on
your lower back roughly in half.

230
00:12:08,362 --> 00:12:10,662
Half? Really?

231
00:12:10,854 --> 00:12:12,917
I didn't measure it,
but it's roughly that.

232
00:12:12,917 --> 00:12:13,558
Give or take.

233
00:12:13,558 --> 00:12:15,382
You haven't measured anything and
you're still throwing out numbers.

234
00:12:15,448 --> 00:12:17,413
Technically, I'm still right.

235
00:12:19,663 --> 00:12:22,287
...Fine. I'll try it.

236
00:12:22,625 --> 00:12:23,583
Whoa, that really is easier!

237
00:12:23,583 --> 00:12:25,227
See? Told you.

238
00:12:25,586 --> 00:12:27,364
It's easier, and I'm a
little annoyed about it.

239
00:12:27,364 --> 00:12:28,400
But it really is easier.

240
00:12:28,447 --> 00:12:29,529
Ten bags left. Two each.

241
00:12:29,529 --> 00:12:30,915
No more arguing about it.

242
00:12:31,019 --> 00:12:32,430
Line the pots up in a row over there.

243
00:12:32,430 --> 00:12:34,495
Yes! Pottun gets the top spot!

244
00:12:34,554 --> 00:12:35,950
They're all the same pot, Tomoe.

245
00:12:43,799 --> 00:12:47,932
One harvest in two months
from this plant? Impossible.

246
00:12:50,297 --> 00:12:52,533
Don't say impossible.

247
00:12:52,533 --> 00:12:53,969
I'm stating facts, Haru.

248
00:12:53,969 --> 00:12:56,554
The roots are packed in, and the
water isn't draining at all.

249
00:12:56,924 --> 00:12:59,527
Even with cramped roots, it's still alive.

250
00:12:59,527 --> 00:13:00,901
Staying alive and bearing
fruit are different things.

251
00:13:00,901 --> 00:13:02,150
We don't have enough days for that.

252
00:13:03,179 --> 00:13:04,473
Then let's buy seedlings.

253
00:13:04,636 --> 00:13:07,753
Even seedlings stall for about
a week after transplanting.

254
00:13:07,765 --> 00:13:08,719
A week?

255
00:13:08,719 --> 00:13:13,220
Then it flowers, gets pollinated,
and the fruit swells.

256
00:13:16,099 --> 00:13:18,564
Each step takes its own time.

257
00:13:18,685 --> 00:13:22,258
Even an early variety needs about
fifty-five days after planting.

258
00:13:22,426 --> 00:13:26,608
Two months is sixty days, so
the margin is only five days.

259
00:13:26,899 --> 00:13:28,573
Five days should be plenty.

260
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:30,226
It isn't.

261
00:13:30,226 --> 00:13:34,717
One stretch of rain and it's over. Pollen
won't set when it's wet like that.

262
00:13:37,015 --> 00:13:40,303
Keep raining and the pollen won't stick.

263
00:13:40,303 --> 00:13:41,591
You worked all that out?

264
00:13:41,635 --> 00:13:43,514
Yes. For a long time.

265
00:13:44,039 --> 00:13:45,923
Then don't call it zero.

266
00:13:45,952 --> 00:13:47,481
I never said zero. I said
the margin is tight.

267
00:13:47,529 --> 00:13:50,157
...If it's not zero, then we do it.

268
00:13:50,333 --> 00:13:52,164
Minori, put that in numbers.

269
00:13:52,179 --> 00:13:53,328
Planting day plus
fifty-five days is harvest.

270
00:13:53,328 --> 00:13:54,890
So the planting date is
the number that matters.

271
00:13:55,193 --> 00:13:57,295
We plant by the eighteenth at the latest.

272
00:13:57,415 --> 00:13:58,529
The eighteenth?

273
00:13:58,906 --> 00:14:00,461
Next week's Saturday. Technically,
that's the eighteenth.

274
00:14:00,461 --> 00:14:01,909
Got it. That's settled.

275
00:14:01,994 --> 00:14:04,503
Wait. Where are we buying the seedlings?

276
00:14:04,571 --> 00:14:07,993
The garden shop on the shopping
street. But they're expensive.

277
00:14:08,325 --> 00:14:10,088
We'll figure out the money.

278
00:14:10,121 --> 00:14:11,439
Stop relying on luck.

279
00:14:11,873 --> 00:14:13,254
It's not luck. It's effort.

280
00:14:13,277 --> 00:14:15,878
...Honestly, either one works for me.

281
00:14:19,104 --> 00:14:20,700
Hey, look at this!

282
00:14:20,952 --> 00:14:24,076
It was at the back of the
shed, in a paper bag.

283
00:14:24,133 --> 00:14:25,201
Huh?

284
00:14:25,682 --> 00:14:27,473
The writing's so faded it's hard to read.

285
00:14:27,529 --> 00:14:28,638
"Akebono Kodama"? Is that right?

286
00:14:28,638 --> 00:14:31,217
What does that mean? Is it a name?

287
00:14:33,908 --> 00:14:35,815
Does that even mean anything?

288
00:14:36,764 --> 00:14:38,033
I see.

289
00:14:38,383 --> 00:14:40,475
Don't go digging through
other people's stuff.

290
00:14:40,724 --> 00:14:43,091
Find a dusty bag like that, and
you still managed to find it.

291
00:14:43,669 --> 00:14:45,408
I wonder if it lists a germination rate.

292
00:14:45,618 --> 00:14:46,684
Nobody sells that variety anymore.

293
00:14:46,684 --> 00:14:48,018
Not for years, not even the big nurseries.

294
00:14:48,035 --> 00:14:49,015
Nobody sells it?

295
00:14:49,018 --> 00:14:51,902
The shop on the shopping
street used to carry it.

296
00:14:53,243 --> 00:14:54,947
So it's rare!

297
00:14:55,008 --> 00:14:57,337
Rare and growable are
different things. Technically.

298
00:14:57,380 --> 00:14:58,829
Are the seeds still alive?

299
00:14:59,323 --> 00:15:00,676
If they were kept dry, there's a chance.

300
00:15:00,676 --> 00:15:01,982
But old seeds lose vigor every year.

301
00:15:02,012 --> 00:15:03,402
That's over ten years old, you know.

302
00:15:03,505 --> 00:15:04,519
Ten years!

303
00:15:05,941 --> 00:15:07,753
It's not about how long they last.

304
00:15:07,753 --> 00:15:09,321
It's whether they sprout.

305
00:15:09,431 --> 00:15:10,350
We're planting them.

306
00:15:10,368 --> 00:15:11,601
Seriously?

307
00:15:11,949 --> 00:15:14,204
Serious. If there's no
other option, we try it.

308
00:15:14,292 --> 00:15:16,545
Let's count them out and
sow them one at a time.

309
00:15:16,581 --> 00:15:17,645
And we're giving them names too!

310
00:15:17,759 --> 00:15:20,065
Don't. That's embarrassing.

311
00:15:20,103 --> 00:15:22,861
Already decided! Number one is Ruby-chan!

312
00:15:22,954 --> 00:15:24,215
Do what you want.

313
00:15:24,786 --> 00:15:26,230
You'll help us, right?

314
00:15:26,312 --> 00:15:27,532
I'll think about it.

315
00:15:34,067 --> 00:15:37,012
What exactly are you
doing up here, Mashiro?

316
00:15:37,611 --> 00:15:38,752
Oh! Principal!

317
00:15:38,812 --> 00:15:41,380
Your voice is carrying all the
way down to the third floor.

318
00:15:41,531 --> 00:15:43,440
I was only talking to the plants!

319
00:15:43,471 --> 00:15:44,282
At full volume?

320
00:15:44,298 --> 00:15:46,255
Yes. So they can hear me.

321
00:15:48,092 --> 00:15:50,835
Plants don't grow faster
because you shout at them.

322
00:15:50,835 --> 00:15:55,822
I want them to know we won't give up.
Loud just feels like it reaches them.

323
00:15:55,931 --> 00:15:58,505
It doesn't reach them. It's just noise.

324
00:15:58,505 --> 00:15:59,723
It's not noise!

325
00:16:01,602 --> 00:16:04,679
If you don't want it called
noise, then be quiet.

326
00:16:07,637 --> 00:16:12,113
After the spring festival, this
roof becomes solar panels.

327
00:16:12,113 --> 00:16:14,551
I know. That's why we're growing it now.

328
00:16:14,638 --> 00:16:16,452
That leaves about three weeks.

329
00:16:16,476 --> 00:16:18,278
The deadline doesn't move.

330
00:16:18,391 --> 00:16:19,167
Yes.

331
00:16:19,266 --> 00:16:23,035
If you're using plants as
proof, show me fruit first.

332
00:16:25,549 --> 00:16:27,703
When it fruits, I'll show you!

333
00:16:27,703 --> 00:16:28,524
Right now there's nothing but leaves.

334
00:16:28,524 --> 00:16:29,390
Leaves mean it's still alive, at least.

335
00:16:29,508 --> 00:16:30,573
I see.

336
00:16:31,030 --> 00:16:33,886
Do you like tomatoes too, Principal?

337
00:16:33,912 --> 00:16:36,716
I don't see what that
has to do with anything.

338
00:16:39,363 --> 00:16:41,745
Earlier you were looking at
the red one, weren't you?

339
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:47,227
I wasn't looking. I was just passing by.

340
00:16:47,250 --> 00:16:49,617
Mashiro, keep your voice down.

341
00:16:49,617 --> 00:16:50,558
Yes...

342
00:16:50,639 --> 00:16:52,716
Wait, you're not angry?

343
00:16:52,716 --> 00:16:55,297
Rules exist so everyone
plays by the same terms.

344
00:16:55,297 --> 00:16:56,771
That includes the club.

345
00:17:15,211 --> 00:17:17,555
Prove it before the deadline.

346
00:17:17,563 --> 00:17:18,707
I will prove it! I promise!

347
00:17:18,707 --> 00:17:19,753
Nobody's giving this up.

348
00:17:19,833 --> 00:17:21,136
Keep it down.

349
00:17:21,248 --> 00:17:23,942
...Yes, Principal.

350
00:17:27,156 --> 00:17:29,330
Ugh, the roots have taken
the shape of the pot.

351
00:17:31,762 --> 00:17:33,959
Pull too hard and they'll snap.

352
00:17:51,007 --> 00:17:51,839
I know!

353
00:17:51,850 --> 00:17:53,573
Gently, gently...

354
00:17:56,256 --> 00:17:58,408
Your hands are surprisingly careful.

355
00:17:58,429 --> 00:17:59,606
Obviously.

356
00:17:59,636 --> 00:18:01,943
Wait, was that a compliment?

357
00:18:03,888 --> 00:18:05,311
Not a compliment. Just an observation.

358
00:18:05,327 --> 00:18:06,688
Loosening the soil now.

359
00:18:09,068 --> 00:18:10,959
Which new soil are we using?

360
00:18:10,959 --> 00:18:13,927
The one Koizumi tested, the
one that drains better.

361
00:18:13,980 --> 00:18:16,685
Drains... Better explain
that to Tomoe later.

362
00:18:16,698 --> 00:18:19,543
The roots are still white. It's alive.

363
00:18:20,066 --> 00:18:22,249
...Maybe.

364
00:18:22,706 --> 00:18:24,157
Did you just say "maybe"?

365
00:18:24,157 --> 00:18:25,400
Didn't say it.

366
00:18:25,417 --> 00:18:26,281
I heard it.

367
00:18:26,373 --> 00:18:29,593
The sky's turning orange. Sunset's pretty.

368
00:18:29,659 --> 00:18:32,181
Evening watering should be light, right?

369
00:18:34,734 --> 00:18:36,845
Just a little. Too much and the roots rot.

370
00:18:36,895 --> 00:18:37,900
Look at you, knowing all that.

371
00:18:37,990 --> 00:18:39,499
I did this before.

372
00:18:39,592 --> 00:18:41,048
Last year's, right?

373
00:18:43,732 --> 00:18:45,137
...Yeah.

374
00:18:45,189 --> 00:18:46,511
It's still fighting
down there in the soil.

375
00:18:46,511 --> 00:18:47,351
It'll come back, I'm sure.

376
00:18:47,469 --> 00:18:49,633
No idea if it'll come back.

377
00:18:49,949 --> 00:18:50,760
The roots are still there.

378
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:52,126
It can still grow back.

379
00:18:53,341 --> 00:18:55,007
Thanks, Riku.

380
00:18:55,083 --> 00:18:56,218
Did you say something?

381
00:18:56,296 --> 00:18:57,551
You heard me, though.

382
00:18:57,551 --> 00:18:59,639
Didn't hear a thing. It's just the wind.

383
00:18:59,639 --> 00:19:01,444
The wind sounds gentle today, doesn't it?

384
00:20:38,979 --> 00:20:40,650
Next week, we're planting seedlings!

385
00:20:40,772 --> 00:20:41,596
Isn't that a little fast?

386
00:20:41,668 --> 00:20:43,370
Whenever you want to
plant is planting time!

387
00:20:43,422 --> 00:20:45,106
Technically, it's best once
the true leaves come out.

388
00:20:45,106 --> 00:20:46,610
Too early, and they
won't survive the move.

389
00:20:46,670 --> 00:20:47,815
Huh?

390
00:20:48,180 --> 00:20:50,857
The forecast says rain, so
someone's getting soaked!

391
00:20:50,857 --> 00:20:52,141
Not me.

392
00:20:52,479 --> 00:20:53,833
It's totally going to be you!

393
00:20:53,833 --> 00:20:55,694
You're the one who always holds the hose.

394
00:20:55,694 --> 00:20:58,498
I don't mind getting wet,
as long as they grow.

395
00:20:59,032 --> 00:21:00,573
Continued on the rooftop, next time.

